# Protocol composition and host qualification

The generic <code>App&lt;P&gt;</code> is assembled around one <Symbol path="upwell::ProtocolDefinition" />. Components and plugins may be protocol-neutral, but the prepared protocol state and built <Symbol path="upwell::ProtocolRuntime" /> are singular.

## Understand the invariant

Preparation follows one protocol type:

1. `P::register` contributes protocol-owned descriptors and scopes.
2. The application registry and DI graph are validated.
3. `P::prepare` validates routes, services, endpoint metadata, or other protocol state.
4. Root components are built.
5. <Symbol path="upwell::PreparedProtocol::build" /> creates exactly one runtime.
6. `App::serve(endpoint)` requires that runtime to implement <code>Serve&lt;E&gt;</code> for the endpoint.

Features decide which protocol definitions can compile. They do not merge prepared definitions.

## Run qualified hosts side by side

```rust plain
let rpc_app = upwell::daemon::App::builder("catalog-rpc")
    .auto_discover()
    .build()
    .await?;
let rpc_shutdown = rpc_app.shutdown_handle();

let http_app = upwell::axum::App::builder("catalog-http")
    .auto_discover()
    .build()
    .await?;
let http_shutdown = http_app.shutdown_handle();

let rpc_task = tokio::spawn(rpc_app.serve(rpc_transport));
let http_task = tokio::spawn(http_app.serve(http_listener));
```

Each host has its own root container, configuration bindings, startup/shutdown sequence, protocol admission limits, and shutdown signal. If one host's failure should stop the other, the supervisor must call the sibling handle and await both tasks.

Do not assume auto-discovery makes every linked controller or service active in both apps. Protocol-specific discovery folds in only variants for the selected definition. Stable IDs and duplicate-path validation remain local to that host's prepared surface.

## Share infrastructure explicitly

Ways to share state include:

- pass pre-built singleton handles into each builder with `with_component`;
- place shared state behind an external database, broker, or service;
- register the same protocol-neutral plugin independently in each app;
- keep jobs in the host whose lifecycle and failure policy should own them.

Avoid sharing a component that assumes one root container or request scope across two roots. A singleton handle may be cloneable while its framework resolution context is not.

## Compose plugins within one host

<Symbol path="upwell_app::Plugin" /> and the application/protocol plugin registrars compose descriptors before validation. They extend one selected protocol; they do not create another runtime. Use explicit plugin registration when ordering or options matter, and inspect the effective plugin plan when contributions collide.

## Qualify endpoint and client code

Side-by-side hosts need separate addresses, paths, and client transports:

```text
public HTTP  -> https://api.example.test
internal RPC -> unix:///run/catalog/catalog.sock
STOMP        -> ws://127.0.0.1:3000/stomp (or wss:// behind a documented TLS deployment)
```

WebSocket protocols are endpoint plugins of the Axum runtime, so registering JSON-WS or STOMP does not violate the one `App<P>` invariant: the selected host is still `App<Axum>`, and Axum owns the upgrades and nested connection/message scopes.

<Callout type="warning" title="No automatic cross-host transaction">

Starting two apps concurrently does not create atomic startup. If the second build or startup fails, explicitly request shutdown for the first and await its cleanup. The per-app partial-startup rollback cannot reach a sibling app.

</Callout>

## Troubleshoot composition

- A service not found in an HTTP host is expected; it belongs to the RPC protocol variant.
- A WebSocket controller is inert until its protocol is mounted on the Axum builder.
- Duplicate route or service failures occur during the corresponding host's preparation, before serving.
- A shutdown request affecting only one listener means the handles were not coordinated by the supervisor.
- A shared resource released too early usually has one app's shutdown hook owning state still used by the other; move ownership to the supervisor or externalize it.

See <GuideRef slug="framework/platforms/features-and-targets">features and targets</GuideRef>, <GuideRef slug="framework/application/lifecycle">lifecycle and shutdown</GuideRef>, <GuideRef slug="rpc/server/policies">RPC server policies</GuideRef>, and <GuideRef slug="axum/websocket">WebSocket protocols</GuideRef>.
